Subject: [PATCH] sync: shuffle the candidates before the stable sort, so ties
 don't always go to the lowest actor ids

Each sync round takes up to desired_count * 2 candidates with
choose_multiple, sorts them by most missing, least recently synced, then
proximity ring, and keeps the first desired_count (3 below 400 members).
With 6 candidates or fewer, choose_multiple returns all of them in the
members map's order, which is by actor id, and the sort is stable.

A node that has just joined ties on every key: it knows of nothing it
misses, has synced with nobody, and has no RTT ring yet (255 for all). So
in small clusters its first rounds always went to its lowest-id peers,
reachable or not, whether or not they had what it lacked. That is a
catch-up delay of tens of seconds, not a stall: once sync timestamps and
rings differ, the order changes. Measured on a new node of a small
cluster: 25-36 s to catch up unpatched, 11-12 s with this patch.

Shuffling before the sort leaves the ordering keys as they are and only
breaks the ties at random.
---
--- a/crates/corro-agent/src/agent/handlers.rs
+++ b/crates/corro-agent/src/agent/handlers.rs
@@ -38,7 +38,7 @@
 use indexmap::map::Entry;
 use indexmap::IndexMap;
 use metrics::{counter, gauge, histogram};
-use rand::{prelude::IteratorRandom, rngs::StdRng, SeedableRng};
+use rand::{prelude::IteratorRandom, rngs::StdRng, seq::SliceRandom, SeedableRng};
 use rangemap::RangeInclusiveSet;
 use serde_json::json;
 use spawn::spawn_counted;
@@ -1234,6 +1234,12 @@
         let mut choices = candidates
             .into_iter()
             .choose_multiple(&mut rng, desired_count * 2);
+        // Break ties at random. With desired_count * 2 candidates or fewer, choose_multiple returns
+        // them all in actor-id order (members.states is a BTreeMap), and the sort below is stable. A
+        // node that has just joined ties on every key (nothing known missing, no sync yet, ring 255),
+        // so without this its first rounds always went to its lowest-id peers, reachable or not,
+        // delaying its catch-up by tens of seconds.
+        choices.shuffle(&mut rng);
 
         choices.sort_by(|a, b| {
             // most missing actors first
